<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Hintata emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">1465 CE – 1525 CE (approx.) · capital Marrakesh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Masmuda Berber emirs of Marrakesh who ruled southern Morocco after the Marinid collapse, until the rising Saadis took their city.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Hintata were a leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masmuda">Masmuda</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">High Atlas</a>, prominent since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> times. Under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate</a> their chiefs served as governors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinid</a> authority collapsed in 1465 the Hintata <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> became de facto independent rulers of the city and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haouz">Haouz</a> plain.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> governed a region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas</a> tribes and plains agriculture from the old imperial capital. They dealt with the Portuguese posts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safi-morocco">Safi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azemmour">Azemmour</a> and are described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-africanus">Leo Africanus</a>, who visited in the early 16th century. Power rested on tribal alliances rather than a strong administration.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate</a>, rising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sous">Sous</a>, pressed northward. The last important emir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-nasir-bu-shentuf">Muhammad ibn Nasir Bu Shentuf</a>, was assassinated in 1524, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saadi-dynasty">Saadis</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> around 1525, making it their capital.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1465 CE – 1525 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:88.86%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1465 CE – 1525 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Marrakesh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad ibn Nasir Bu Shentuf</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (Maliki)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Berber, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hintata, Emirate of Marrakesh</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1495&amp;focus=hintata-emirate" aria-label="Hintata emirate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4474 1028 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hintata emirate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4794 1284L4873 1200M4794 1284L4873 1200"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4873 1200h0M4873 1200h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4794 1284h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4794 1284h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Marrakesh. <a href="/atlas?year=1495&amp;focus=hintata-emirate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
